    {
      "id": "44ee83e261198431c31cd10da69b931f0a15124c",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1342296842,
          "type": null,
          "body": "\"an internet\" vs \"the Internet\".  \nShould it be \"Plugin\" when talking about code that uses the plugin framework.  \nOr UpperCamelCase as in [filesystem.c](https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/blob/master/wsutil/filesystem.c#L246):  \n>  * It also says that the \"PlugIns\" directory  \n  \nand [pluginifdemo_about.ui](https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/blob/master/plugins/epan/pluginifdemo/ui/pluginifdemo_about.ui#L99):  \n> This PlugIn demonstrates functionality of the interface API for plugins and extcap interfaces  \n  \nAs is often the case with the Wireshark code (code in general?) this started with a compiler error in `pluginifdemo`. While searching through the [WSDG](https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsdg_html/) with \"plugin\" to see what documentation is available, the search hit the \"versatile plugin\" mention w.r.t. extcap. That just seemed out of place when compared to the rest of the places \"plugin\" is used. I don't think the WSDG is for seasoned developers (although it wouldn't hurt). Not being the brightest bulb on the tree, I try to look at everything like someone that is new to the project.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6701257,
            "username": "chuckcraft",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Chuck Craft",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0809ffc6d9d98013a1895132bc055c622830b8a20bfa49328f53d5eb4a1206cb?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/chuckcraft"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-04-05T12:56:41.413Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-04-05T12:56:41.413Z",
